\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{A local feature that persists under small perturbation.} In ingoing Schwarzschild coordinates write $g=-f(r)dv^2+2dv\,dr+r^2d\Omega^2$, $f(r)=1-2M/r$. Choose future null normals $\ell=\partial_v+\tfrac12f\partial_r$ and $n=-\partial_r$, normalized by $g(\ell,n)=-1$. Since a sphere has area $4\pi r^2$, their expansions are
\[
 \theta_\ell=2\ell(r)/r=f(r)/r,\qquad \theta_n=2n(r)/r=-2/r.
\]
At any fixed $0<r_0<2M$, both are strictly negative. Their maximum is bounded above by a negative constant on the compact sphere.

\paragraph{A precise limited stability statement.} The expansions depend continuously on the metric, its first derivatives, the embedded sphere and its first two derivatives, once the null normals are chosen continuously. Therefore every sufficiently small perturbation in those norms retains negativity at this sphere. This follows by taking the perturbation of each expansion less than half its strict negative margin. In initial-data language the same observation follows from the expressions involving the sphere's mean curvature and the trace of the second fundamental form.

\paragraph{Global obstruction.} This proves persistence of a strictly trapped surface only when the chosen initial slice already contains one and the specified local norms are controlled. It does not show completeness of future null infinity, establish a globally visible exterior, or identify the event horizon. Connecting trapping to a black-hole region requires global causal and future-development information, precisely what a nonlinear stability theorem must provide. No smallness topology for all source data has been supplied, and neither of the two global conclusions in the original question is proved by this local calculation.

